Let me think about the logic more carefully. The function `SimplifyDemandedUseFPClass` is trying to simplify a value `V` given that we demand certain floating-point classes (`DemandedMask`). It returns a simplified value or nullptr.

In `visitReturnInst`, `DemandedMask` is `~ReturnClass`. So if `ReturnClass` excludes NaN, then `DemandedMask` includes everything EXCEPT NaN. The function tries to simplify the return value under the assumption that it can't be NaN.

The potential bug could be in how `SimplifyDemandedUseFPClass` handles certain operations. Let me look at the select case again (lines 1978-1991):

```cpp
case Instruction::Select: {
  KnownFPClass KnownLHS, KnownRHS;
  if (SimplifyDemandedFPClass(I, 2, DemandedMask, KnownRHS, Depth + 1) ||
      SimplifyDemandedFPClass(I, 1, DemandedMask, KnownLHS, Depth + 1))
    return I;

  if (KnownLHS.isKnownNever(DemandedMask))
    return I->getOperand(2);
  if (KnownRHS.isKnownNever(DemandedMask))
    return I->getOperand(1);

  // TODO: Recognize clamping patterns
  Known = KnownLHS | KnownRHS;
  break;
}
```

The logic says: if LHS is known to NEVER have any class in `DemandedMask`, then return RHS. But `DemandedMask` is what we demand (what's allowed). So if LHS never has any allowed class, then LHS must always be in the forbidden classes. But wait, that doesn't make sense because the function has `nofpclass` attribute that forbids certain classes.

Actually, I think the logic might be inverted! Let me re-read: `isKnownNever(DemandedMask)` returns true if the value is known to NEVER be in any of the classes in `DemandedMask`. So if `DemandedMask` is "everything except NaN", and LHS is known to never be in "everything except NaN", then LHS must always be NaN (or in the complement of `DemandedMask`).

But if the function has `nofpclass="nan"`, then returning a value that's always NaN would be wrong! The function should return poison instead.

Actually, looking at line 1908-1909 again:
```cpp
if (DemandedMask == fcNone)
  return isa<UndefValue>(V) ? nullptr : PoisonValue::get(VTy);
```

If `DemandedMask` is empty (fcNone), it returns poison. But in the select case, if `KnownLHS.isKnownNever(DemandedMask)` is true, it returns `I->getOperand(2)` (RHS), not poison!

So here's a potential bug: If `DemandedMask` is empty (all classes are forbidden), and we have a select where LHS is known to never satisfy the empty mask (which is always true for any value!), then the code would return RHS. But actually, if all classes are forbidden, the function should return poison (or undef).

Wait, but `isKnownNever(fcNone)` should return true for any value, because no value can be in the empty set. So the condition `KnownLHS.isKnownNever(DemandedMask)` would always be true when `DemandedMask == fcNone`. This seems wrong!

Let me test this hypothesis:

